(window.LADDER_CARDS["46·eng"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["46·eng"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"46·eng.1.1","q":"What does a hub do with a signal? Why must two machines that clash wait at random?","a":"It copies every signal it gets to every other port, so all the machines on it share one wire and two that send at once garble each other; each must notice the clash and try again after a random wait, since equal waits would clash again","w":["It sends the signal to the one port that needs it; clashes cannot happen","It stores the signal and sends it later; they wait at random to save power","It drops every second signal; they wait a fixed time, the same for both"],"why":"Try again, as resending does."},
 {"u":"46·eng.2.1","q":"What does the learning-switch technique say? Why do switches in a loop agree on a tree?","a":"A switch that writes down the port each sender’s address arrived on sends every later message for that address only out of that port, and until it has seen an address it sends that message to every port; switches joined in a loop would pass such a message round and round for ever, so they agree on a tree through the links and switch off the rest, a tree having exactly one path between any two points","w":["A switch knows every address from the start; a tree is agreed because it is the shortest network","A switch sends every message to every port always; a tree is agreed to share the load","A switch asks a central list for each address; they agree on a tree because loops are slower by one hop"],"why":"Other ports are free for other talk at the same time."},
 {"u":"46·eng.3.1","q":"How does a router choose where to send a message? What does each layer add?","a":"It picks the next router on a shortest path, and routers trade what they know of the map, so each learns the paths without a central list; the link moves bits between neighbours, the internet layer carries numbered messages from network to network with no promise of arrival, and the transport layer adds handshakes and resends on top","w":["It asks the sender for the path; each layer adds a promise of delivery","It sends to every neighbour; the link layer adds handshakes and the transport layer moves bits","It follows a central list; the internet layer promises arrival and the transport layer adds addresses"],"why":"The layers stack."},
 {"u":"46·eng.4.1","q":"How does a switch come to know an address? Which layer promises delivery?","a":"It learns addresses from the messages it sees; and the internet layer does its best: promises come from the layer above, the transport layer","w":["It knows every address from the start; and the internet layer promises delivery","From a central list; and the link layer promises delivery","By asking each machine in turn; and no layer adds anything"],"why":"Both are rows of the step’s Moves to Watch Out For."});
/* end phase F und_r45 */
